    "title": "Cold front barreling toward Texas to plummet temperatures",
    "text": "Mist on small asphalt country road as Texas prepares for the next cold front. Lucas Ninno/Getty ImagesAnother late-season cold front is barreling toward Texas, and it’s set to send temperatures plummeting. Some cities will dip back down to freezing while others will get a milder cool down.It may not exactly feel like winter for most of Texas early next week, but cooler days will feel a lot more like fall than three weeks into meteorological spring. Some northern sections of the Lone Star State will dip down to freezing again, while more central and southern swaths of Texas will get a reprieve from 90-degree days.Article continues below this adUp in the Texas Panhandle, cities near the Texas-Oklahoma border will see a whopping nearly 20-degree dip in afternoon highs between Saturday, March 21, and Sunday afternoon. By Sunday evening, lows will creep back down toward freezing again, hitting 32 degrees.“A change up in the weather has an increasing chance of occurring on Sunday as a weather system flattens out and then pushes away the high pressure from the southern plains,” the National Weather Service Amarillo office warns. “This would bring a noticeable dip in the heat that will be baking the panhandles.”This could front should only linger over the Texas Panhandle for a couple days, as it makes its way southeast and temperatures begin to rise again by Wednesday. This burst of arctic air should reach the greater Dallas-Fort Worth metroplex by Monday.Article continues below this adA roughly 12-degree drop in afternoon highs is expected in DFW between Sunday and Monday afternoons, and the metro area should see a few days in the low 80s – a welcome respite from a week hovering in the 90s.Around the same time, San Antonio, and most of South Central Texas, should see a brief cooling down as well. This second-wave cold front will keep temperatures from soaring in the Alamo City after dipping into winter-like territory over the pas week.Article continues below this adThis isn’t expected to be the unusual and, at times, extreme front of last week, however. No rain or severe weather is expected, though models are still early, and afternoons should linger in the upper 80s across San Antonio before dipping into the upper 50s and low 60s overnight.",
